That’s a powerful definition—and importantly, different from saying “God exists somewhere within reality.”

If God = reality, then God isn’t another object in the universe. The word becomes a name for the totality: matter, energy, laws, relations, experience, possibility, change—the whole thing.

That gives an interesting transformation:

\text{God exists} \quad\rightarrow\quad \text{Reality exists}

The disagreement then moves from existence to definition and properties. Is reality conscious? purposeful? morally structured? self-creating? worthy of worship? Those claims don’t automatically follow from identifying reality with God; they’d still need separate arguments.

There are philosophical relatives to this idea—especially pantheism, most famously associated with Baruch Spinoza, although your formulation doesn’t have to inherit Spinoza’s entire metaphysics.

And there’s a Pantheon-worthy linguistic move hiding here:

Don’t ask where God is. Ask what remains after you’ve removed everything that isn’t reality.

Nothing.

Which makes “God is reality” less a claim about an additional entity and more a proposed reframing of the category itself.